# Java `this` vs `super`

In Java, `this` and `super` are keywords used mainly when working with **objects, constructors, and inheritance**.

The easiest way to remember them is:

```text
this  → current object
super → parent class
```

---

# 1. What is `this`?

`this` refers to the **current object**.

Example:

```java
class Student {

    String name;

    Student(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }
}
```

Here we have two `name` variables:

```java
String name;
```

and:

```java
Student(String name)
```

The parameter `name` hides the instance variable.

So:

```java
this.name
```

means:

> The `name` belonging to the current object.

And:

```java
name
```

means:

> The `name` parameter.

Therefore:

```java
this.name = name;
```

means:

```text
object's name = parameter's name
```

---

# 2. Why Do We Use `this`?

Without `this`, the code can become confusing when a parameter has the same name as an instance variable.

Example:

```java
class Student {

    String name;

    Student(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }
}
```

Create an object:

```java
Student s1 = new Student("Nikitha");
```

Now:

```text
s1.name
   ↓
"Nikitha"
```

---

# 3. `this` Can Call Another Constructor

`this()` can be used to call another constructor of the **same class**.

Example:

```java
class Student {

    String name;
    int age;

    Student() {
        this("Unknown", 0);
    }

    Student(String name, int age) {
        this.name = name;
        this.age = age;
    }
}
```

When we write:

```java
Student s = new Student();
```

the first constructor calls:

```java
this("Unknown", 0);
```

which calls the second constructor.

So:

```text
Student()
   ↓
this("Unknown", 0)
   ↓
Student(String, int)
```

---

# 4. What is `super`?

`super` refers to the **parent class part of the current object**.

Suppose:

```java
class Person {

    String name = "Person";
}
```

and:

```java
class Student extends Person {

    String name = "Student";
}
```

Now:

```java
Student s = new Student();

System.out.println(s.name);
```

Output:

```text
Student
```

Because the child class has its own `name`.

But:

```java
System.out.println(super.name);
```

refers to the parent's `name`.

Output:

```text
Person
```

---

# 5. `this` vs `super`

```text
this
 ↓
Current class / current object

super
 ↓
Parent class
```

Example:

```java
class Person {

    String name = "Parent";
}

class Student extends Person {

    String name = "Child";

    void display() {

        System.out.println(this.name);
        System.out.println(super.name);
    }
}
```

Output:

```text
Child
Parent
```

---

# 6. `super()` Calls Parent Constructor

`super()` is used to call the constructor of the parent class.

Example:

```java
class Person {

    Person() {
        System.out.println("Person constructor");
    }
}

class Student extends Person {

    Student() {
        super();

        System.out.println("Student constructor");
    }
}
```

Create the object:

```java
Student s = new Student();
```

Output:

```text
Person constructor
Student constructor
```

The parent constructor executes first.

---

# 7. Why Does Parent Constructor Run First?

When creating a child object, the parent part of the object must be initialized first.

Think:

```text
new Student()
     ↓
Person constructor
     ↓
Student constructor
     ↓
Student object ready
```

This is why constructor execution follows the inheritance hierarchy.

---

# 8. `super` Can Call Parent Methods

Suppose:

```java
class Person {

    void display() {
        System.out.println("Person");
    }
}
```

Child class:

```java
class Student extends Person {

    void display() {
        System.out.println("Student");

        super.display();
    }
}
```

Now:

```java
Student s = new Student();

s.display();
```

Output:

```text
Student
Person
```

Here:

```java
super.display();
```

explicitly calls the parent version of the method.

---

# 9. Complete Example

```java
class Person {

    String name;

    Person(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }

    void display() {
        System.out.println("Name: " + name);
    }
}
```

Child class:

```java
class Student extends Person {

    int marks;

    Student(String name, int marks) {

        super(name);

        this.marks = marks;
    }

    void display() {

        super.display();

        System.out.println("Marks: " + this.marks);
    }
}
```

Create the object:

```java
Student s = new Student("Nikitha", 90);

s.display();
```

Output:

```text
Name: Nikitha
Marks: 90
```

Here we used both keywords:

```java
super(name);
```

to call the parent constructor.

And:

```java
this.marks = marks;
```

to refer to the current object's `marks`.

---

# 10. `this()` vs `super()`

This is very important.

| Keyword | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `this` | Current object |
| `super` | Parent class |
| `this()` | Calls another constructor in the same class |
| `super()` | Calls parent class constructor |
| `this.variable` | Current object's variable |
| `super.variable` | Parent class variable |
| `this.method()` | Current class method |
| `super.method()` | Parent class method |

---

# 11. Important Rule

`this()` and `super()` must be the **first statement inside a constructor**.

Correct:

```java
Student() {
    super();
    System.out.println("Student");
}
```

Correct:

```java
Student() {
    this("Unknown");
}
```

You cannot do:

```java
Student() {

    System.out.println("Hello");

    super();
}
```

because `super()` must come first.

---

# 12. Easy Mental Model

Imagine inheritance like a family.

```text
Person
   ↑
Student
   ↑
CollegeStudent
```

Then:

```text
this
 ↓
"I am talking about myself."

super
 ↓
"I am talking about my parent."
```

So:

```java
this.name
```

means:

> My name.

And:

```java
super.name
```

means:

> My parent's name.

---

# Key Takeaways

- `this` refers to the **current object**.
- `super` refers to the **parent class**.
- `this()` calls another constructor in the same class.
- `super()` calls the parent class constructor.
- `this.variable` accesses the current object's variable.
- `super.variable` accesses the parent's variable.
- `this.method()` calls the current class method.
- `super.method()` calls the parent class method.
- `this()` and `super()` must be the first statement in a constructor.

---

## One-Line Summary

> **`this` is used to refer to the current object, while `super` is used to access the parent class.**
